[00:17:19] Clustara: [review] rejected — 리뷰 거절: internal/analyzer/resourcesummary.go:42-47 PodResourceNumbers now applies the init-container max rule to LIMITS as well as requests. The max rule is correct for scheduling/capacity/cost (what the PR set out to fix), but LimCPUm/LimMemB feed runtime consumers that compare *running-container* metrics against them, so the pod is now measured against a limit no running container enforces. Concrete: pod with initContainer 'load-model' limits memory 8Gi and container 'app' limits memory 1Gi. main returns LimMemB=1Gi (the value the app container actually OOMKills at); HEAD returns 8Gi. The PR's own new test (internal/analyzer/capacity_effective_request_test.go:75) asserts exactly this new behavior (init 2Gi wins over app 512Mi), so it is confirmed, not hypothetical. / internal/analyzer/predictive_incident.go:52,60 consequence of the above: PodCPUSaturation / PodMemorySaturation and the 24h PredictedCPU/MemoryExhaustion drafts divide pod-level metric usage by q.LimCPUm / q.LimMemB. With app limit 1Gi and an init limit of 8Gi, a pod sitting at 950Mi used to trip the 0.9 ratio and emit a critical 'PodMemorySaturation' draft; it now computes 950Mi/8Gi = 0.12 and emits nothing. The alert is silently lost, with no test covering it and no mention in scripts/changelog.txt. / internal/proxy/admin_k8s_resource_advisor.go:81 -> internal/analyzer/resourceadvisor.go:86,129 same root cause on the advisor path: the CPU-throttling rule (UsageCPUm >= LimCPUm*throttleNearLimit) stops firing when an init container declares a larger CPU limit than the app containers, and the OOMKilled recommendation renders rec.Current = FormatMemBytes(in.LimMemB) — i.e. it tells the operator the pod's 'current memory limit' is the init container's 8Gi while the container that just OOMKilled is capped at 1Gi.
https://github.com/hkjang/clustara/pull/11#issuecomment-5552758147
